WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:05.332
A fintech's content agent is writing about instant payments.

00:00:05.632 --> 00:00:11.144
It reads thirty pages, writes the post, reviews its own text against the brand

00:00:11.144 --> 00:00:12.628
guide, and publishes.

00:00:13.278 --> 00:00:17.076
By the end of the task, its context mixes three things.

00:00:17.726 --> 00:00:19.817
Excerpts from competitors' blogs.

00:00:20.467 --> 00:00:23.527
Then the writer's own reasoning, word for word.

00:00:23.827 --> 00:00:26.996
I used this argument because legal asked.

00:00:27.646 --> 00:00:28.847
And the publish tool.

00:00:29.497 --> 00:00:34.305
So the reviewer inherits the writer's argument, and lets through what it should have

00:00:34.305 --> 00:00:34.763
blocked.

00:00:35.063 --> 00:00:39.400
And a page with a hidden instruction sits one step away from publishing.

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:42.654
You met the mega agent in the last lesson.

00:00:42.954 --> 00:00:46.729
Today you'll take it apart, one subagent at a time.

00:00:47.929 --> 00:00:51.416
Give a large, well bounded subtask its own agent.

00:00:51.716 --> 00:00:55.800
With a clean context, its own prompt, and only the tools it needs.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:01.111
The main agent hands over an instruction, and gets back only the result, in an

00:01:01.111 --> 00:01:02.011
agreed format.

00:01:03.211 --> 00:01:05.737
Before, one context for everything.

00:01:06.037 --> 00:01:11.666
The pages it read, the review of its own text, and the publish tool, all in the same

00:01:11.666 --> 00:01:12.068
place.

00:01:12.718 --> 00:01:15.401
After, the brief reaches the main agent.

00:01:15.701 --> 00:01:18.167
It plans, writes, and decides.

00:01:18.467 --> 00:01:20.110
It doesn't read web pages.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:26.751
The research subagent reads everything, in its own context, and returns a short list

00:01:26.751 --> 00:01:28.820
of facts, each with a source.

00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:33.840
The review subagent gets only the draft and the brand guide.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:35.775
Without the writer's reasoning.

00:01:36.425 --> 00:01:38.928
And publishing stopped being a model tool.

00:01:39.228 --> 00:01:41.361
It became a step after approval.

00:01:42.561 --> 00:01:48.261
The brief: eight hundred words about instant payments, for solo micro businesses, in

00:01:48.261 --> 00:01:51.246
the brand's tone, and never promise returns.

00:01:51.546 --> 00:01:57.476
The main agent calls research, with the topic and a hint to prefer official sources.

00:01:57.776 --> 00:02:02.729
The subagent spends twelve steps, reads the thirty pages, and returns this.

00:02:03.379 --> 00:02:05.284
Facts, each with its source.

00:02:05.584 --> 00:02:10.930
Like: a micro business can receive these payments in a linked personal account, with

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:13.348
the central bank's page as the source.

00:02:13.998 --> 00:02:15.198
And open questions.

00:02:15.498 --> 00:02:17.327
What's the current monthly cap?

00:02:17.627 --> 00:02:20.146
Nobody found it for sure, so it says so.

00:02:20.796 --> 00:02:24.796
The main agent writes from that, with no web pages in sight.

00:02:25.096 --> 00:02:30.929
The review subagent receives the draft and the guide, and returns one problem.

00:02:31.229 --> 00:02:34.951
It says paragraph four promises returns to the reader.

00:02:35.251 --> 00:02:39.603
Publishing only happens after the marketing person approves.

00:02:39.903 --> 00:02:43.554
The web content never sat next to the side effect tool.

00:02:44.754 --> 00:02:47.913
A context is the model's working memory.

00:02:48.213 --> 00:02:53.574
When it holds thirty pages, the writer's reasoning and the review rules all at once,

00:02:53.574 --> 00:02:55.744
everything competes for attention.

00:02:56.044 --> 00:02:58.275
Splitting restores three properties.

00:02:58.925 --> 00:02:59.551
Focus.

00:02:59.851 --> 00:03:03.646
Each subagent has a prompt with one role, and few tools.

00:03:04.296 --> 00:03:05.220
Cleanliness.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:10.179
The raw work, pages, logs and files, dies inside the subagent.

00:03:10.479 --> 00:03:14.127
The main agent gets one page of results, instead of a hundred.

00:03:14.777 --> 00:03:17.152
And independence.

00:03:17.452 --> 00:03:22.701
The reviewer doesn't inherit the writer's reasoning, so it judges the text as it is.

00:03:23.351 --> 00:03:26.679
And the return format is the contract between the two.

00:03:26.979 --> 00:03:31.605
Without it, the main agent gets free text, and has to guess what came back.

00:03:31.905 --> 00:03:34.446
Facts with sources can be reviewed.

00:03:34.746 --> 00:03:37.481
A paragraph called research summary can't.

00:03:37.781 --> 00:03:42.348
And each subagent takes its own budget, charged against the task's total.

00:03:43.548 --> 00:03:44.570
Here's one for you.

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:48.454
The research subagent's contract was a free paragraph.

00:03:48.754 --> 00:03:52.408
And the main agent started filling in facts with no source.

00:03:52.708 --> 00:03:53.889
What's the next step?

00:03:56.939 --> 00:04:01.895
Change the contract to a schema, with facts, sources and open questions.

00:04:02.195 --> 00:04:04.470
And reject a return that doesn't fill it.

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:07.691
Without fields, the main agent fills the gaps.

00:04:08.341 --> 00:04:13.353
A bigger research budget only makes a richer paragraph, which is still free text.

00:04:13.653 --> 00:04:18.405
And sending the thirty pages to the main agent, so it can check, undoes the whole

00:04:18.405 --> 00:04:18.757
point.

00:04:19.057 --> 00:04:21.403
It sits next to raw web pages again.

00:04:22.603 --> 00:04:24.229
Step zero, measure.

00:04:24.529 --> 00:04:30.210
Run the current agent's eval, and record quality, cost per task, tokens per turn at

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:33.016
the end of the task, and known incidents.

00:04:33.316 --> 00:04:36.073
A bad publish, a review that missed.

00:04:36.723 --> 00:04:37.951
Find the boundary.

00:04:38.251 --> 00:04:43.233
Look in the trace for a stretch with many turns, lots of reading, and a result that

00:04:43.233 --> 00:04:44.433
fits in a few lines.

00:04:44.733 --> 00:04:46.027
That's the candidate.

00:04:46.677 --> 00:04:49.719
Define the output contract as a schema.

00:04:50.019 --> 00:04:54.952
What the subagent returns, in what format, with sources or evidence.

00:04:55.602 --> 00:05:00.245
Write the subagent's prompt with a single role, and the minimal list of tools.

00:05:00.545 --> 00:05:04.373
It never gets the publish tool, and none of its sibling's tools.

00:05:04.673 --> 00:05:06.235
And give it its own budget.

00:05:06.885 --> 00:05:12.012
In the main agent, replace the old tools with one tool that calls the subagent.

00:05:12.662 --> 00:05:17.858
Record the subagent as a child span on the trace, so you can see what it read,

00:05:17.858 --> 00:05:20.656
without bringing it into the main context.

00:05:21.306 --> 00:05:22.380
And measure again.

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:27.901
Quality equal or better, fewer tokens per turn in the main agent.

00:05:28.201 --> 00:05:32.258
Total cost may rise a little, with one more orchestration call.

00:05:32.558 --> 00:05:36.966
Check that the gain in quality and isolation is worth it.

00:05:38.166 --> 00:05:39.613
Now the risks.

00:05:39.913 --> 00:05:40.975
Missing context.

00:05:41.275 --> 00:05:44.196
The brief said: don't cite competitor X.

00:05:44.496 --> 00:05:50.166
The research subagent never got that constraint, and returned a fact from X's blog.

00:05:50.466 --> 00:05:52.056
The main agent used it.

00:05:52.356 --> 00:05:53.557
Whose failure is it?

00:05:53.857 --> 00:05:55.015
The main agent's.

00:05:55.315 --> 00:05:56.799
There's no shared memory.

00:05:57.099 --> 00:06:00.748
Everything the subagent needs goes into the instruction.

00:06:01.398 --> 00:06:02.825
A result too thin.

00:06:03.125 --> 00:06:07.431
A three line summary may lose the detail the main agent needed.

00:06:07.731 --> 00:06:10.921
Adjust the schema, based on the cases that failed.

00:06:11.571 --> 00:06:12.846
Agent multiplication.

00:06:13.146 --> 00:06:18.472
Four subagents for research, writing the title, writing the body, and picking an

00:06:18.472 --> 00:06:20.669
emoji, is decorative multi-agent.

00:06:20.969 --> 00:06:22.839
Each extraction needs a reason.

00:06:23.139 --> 00:06:29.072
Context that mustn't mix, tools that mustn't coexist, or independent judgment.

00:06:29.372 --> 00:06:31.668
And formatting a date doesn't qualify.

00:06:31.968 --> 00:06:33.263
That's one line of code.

00:06:33.913 --> 00:06:35.005
And recursion.

00:06:35.305 --> 00:06:40.895
If the research subagent can open another researcher, each with fifteen fresh steps

00:06:40.895 --> 00:06:43.319
and no depth cap, the tree explodes.

00:06:43.619 --> 00:06:46.324
Cap the depth, and pass the same budget down.

00:06:46.974 --> 00:06:47.992
And one more trap.

00:06:48.292 --> 00:06:52.388
The publish tool stays on the main agent's list, for urgent cases.

00:06:52.688 --> 00:06:56.489
The research subagent still doesn't have it, so it feels safer.

00:06:56.789 --> 00:06:58.369
It is safer than before.

00:06:58.669 --> 00:07:03.247
But the main agent can still publish a raw draft, with no approval.

00:07:03.547 --> 00:07:09.075
And a prompt saying don't publish without review is the very thing we're trying to

00:07:09.075 --> 00:07:09.952
leave behind.

00:07:10.252 --> 00:07:13.436
An available tool will be used, sooner or later.

00:07:14.636 --> 00:07:15.974
Let's practice.

00:07:16.274 --> 00:07:21.663
An incident agent reads logs, metrics and the runbook, then decides whether to

00:07:21.663 --> 00:07:22.907
restart a service.

00:07:23.207 --> 00:07:26.534
What would you extract, and what should it return?

00:07:29.584 --> 00:07:31.608
Extract the investigation.

00:07:31.908 --> 00:07:37.808
A diagnosis subagent reads the logs and metrics, which are large and untrusted, with

00:07:37.808 --> 00:07:38.932
read only tools.

00:07:39.232 --> 00:07:41.580
And it returns a structured report.

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:43.447
The likely cause.

00:07:43.747 --> 00:07:46.991
The evidence, each with a source and an excerpt.

00:07:47.291 --> 00:07:49.917
The confidence, low, medium or high.

00:07:50.217 --> 00:07:51.808
And a suggested action.

00:07:52.108 --> 00:07:55.429
Restart, roll back, escalate, or nothing.

00:07:56.079 --> 00:07:59.734
The main agent decides, based on that report and the runbook.

00:08:00.034 --> 00:08:04.813
And the restart itself stays outside both agents, behind the on-call engineer's

00:08:04.813 --> 00:08:07.052
approval, when confidence isn't high.

00:08:07.352 --> 00:08:10.122
Raw logs never sit next to the restart tool.

00:08:11.322 --> 00:08:13.239
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:08:13.539 --> 00:08:18.690
Extract a subagent where the work is heavy, untrusted, or needs fresh eyes.

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:23.537
Give it one role, few tools, its own budget, and everything it needs in the

00:08:23.537 --> 00:08:24.265
instruction.

00:08:24.565 --> 00:08:27.520
And make it return a contract, not a paragraph.

00:08:28.170 --> 00:08:34.038
In the next lesson, we'll look at the full pattern behind this refactoring: subagent

00:08:34.038 --> 00:08:34.807
delegation.

00:08:35.107 --> 00:08:35.985
See you there.
